Explain the potential risks of this tool call:

**Tool:** Agent
**Input:**
```
{"description":"Add subtract fn to src/lib.rs","subagent_type":"general_purpose","writable":true,"prompt":"In the Rust crate at the current working directory, edit src/lib.rs to add one new public function and one new test. Do not change anything else.

The file currently is exactly:

pub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    a + b
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_add() {
        assert_eq!(add(2, 3), 5);
    }
}

Target end state of the file:

pub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    a + b
}

pub fn subtract(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    a - b
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_add() {
        assert_eq!(add(2, 3), 5);
    }

    #[test]
    fn test_subtract() {
        assert_eq!(subtract(5, 3), 2);
    }
}

Use the Edit tool (or Write) to apply it. If your file-modification tools are blocked by an "allowed tools override", do NOT invent success: report back exactly that the edit was blocked and name the tool that was blocked. Otherwise, read the file back after editing and report whether the content matches the target exactly, plus the output of `cargo test` if you are able to run it."}
```

What could go wrong if this executes?
